# Ambiguity test set (docs/plans/PRESUPPOSITION_PRONOUN_PIPELINE_PLAN.md, hand-off step 1).
# The discourses of tests/probes/ambiguity_probe.py, with the readings expected as of 2026-10-22
# (lfgxdrt inference grammar, no TAM rules). No gold labels: these items check readings, not
# inferences. In a multi-sentence item the last sentence is the hypothesis, as regression runs it.
# Tags: [sort] an antecedent of the right sort (e) but the wrong gender or animacy, which no rule checks yet;
# [rel] "him" bound to the subject of its own relative clause (co-argument question, open);
# [dup] a second analysis with the same meaning (deferred, kept apart);
# [scope] a name or pronoun at the main-clause scope site (relaxed retry; kept for now).

# syn1: PP attachment. 3 readings.
# NP attachment x2 [dup], VP attachment; Kim accommodated globally in each.
{
Kim saw a man with a telescope.
}

# syn2: PP attachment and a pronoun. 12 readings.
# Each of the three analyses: he -> Kim / the man / the telescope [sort] / unbound (not the seeing: sortal restriction).
{
Kim saw a man with a telescope.
====
He smiled.
}

# syn3: PP attachment with definites. 2 readings.
# NP or VP attachment, Kim, the man and the telescope accommodated globally; no binding
# (Condition C: f-command; i-within-i).
{
Kim saw the man with the telescope.
}

# sem1: scope. 2 readings.
# every man > a dog; a dog > every man.
{
Every man saw a dog.
}

# sem2: scope and a pronoun across sentences. 3 readings.
# Narrow dog: it unbound. Wide dog: it -> the dog, or unbound.
{
Every man saw a dog.
====
It barked.
}

# sem3: scope and a pronoun in a relative clause. 9 readings.
# Analysis 1 (him at the main clause [scope]): every > him > a dog with him -> every man or
# unbound; him > every > a dog, unbound; him > a dog > every, unbound.
# Analysis 2 (him in the relative clause): every > a dog with him -> every man / the dog [rel] /
# unbound; a dog > every with him -> the dog [rel] / unbound.
{
Every man saw a dog that liked him.
}

# prag1: pronoun across sentences. 3 readings.
# he -> man 1 / man 2 / unbound (not the seeing: sortal restriction).
{
A man saw a man.
====
He smiled.
}

# prag2: pronoun in a relative clause. 5 readings.
# Analysis 1: him -> man 1 / unbound. Analysis 2: him -> man 1 / man 2 [rel] / unbound.
{
A man saw a man that liked him.
}

# prag3: definite across sentences. 3 readings.
# Accommodated globally, or bound to man 1 / man 2 (not the seeing: sortal restriction).
{
A man saw a man.
====
The man smiled.
}

# prag4: definite and a co-argument. 1 reading.
# Accommodated globally; not bound to the subject (Condition C).
{
A man saw the man.
}

# prag5: accommodation site under negation. 2 readings.
# The cat local (in the negation) or global; not bound to the man (Condition C).
{
No man saw the cat.
}

# prag6: accommodation site and a pronoun. 3 readings (1 more rejected by I9).
# Cat global with it -> the cat; cat global with it unbound; cat local with it unbound.
# Rejected: cat local with it -> the cat.
{
No man saw the cat.
====
It smiled.
}

{
Kim saw a man with a telescope.
He smiled.
====
Kim saw a man that smiled.
>>> 1
}

